  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Michael Boyden\u2019s <em>Predicting the Past</em> is showing an early but meaningful scholarly footprint. The book has 7 OpenAlex citations, 568 recorded downloads from OAPEN, and two Crossref-indexed mentions in public discourse, suggesting that it is being read, cited, and discussed within its field.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2021, <em>Predicting the Past</em> advances a reflexive account of American literary history and the institutional dynamics that shape literary studies. Its conceptual focus on legitimation, misrepresentation, and the history of literature aligns with the kinds of questions that have kept the book visible across adjacent scholarly conversations in literary criticism, sociology, and epistemology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still modest, but it is already distributed across several venues and formats, including journal articles and book chapters. That pattern suggests the book is being taken up as a framing work in discussions of American literature, historiography, and the discipline of literary studies rather than only as a narrowly cited specialist monograph.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data points to broader reader attention: the book has been downloaded 568 times through OAPEN, with activity appearing steadily across the year rather than as a single isolated spike. Because the title is available open access, this download record is especially relevant as evidence of direct readership and discovery.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also limited but visible public discourse around the work, with Crossref-indexed mentions indicating some circulation beyond citation alone. Overall, the record shows a book that is beginning to establish scholarly traction while still in an early phase of accumulation, with reading and discussion outpacing the current citation count in evidentiary breadth.</p>",
